{"causes": ["The institutional reforms and meritocratic opportunities opened by the French Revolution allowed talented individuals like Champagny to rise rapidly in public service.", "Napoleon Bonaparte's consolidation of power after the Consulate created a demand for loyal and capable administrators to implement his ambitious domestic and foreign policies.", "Champagny's early naval career and experience abroad provided him with a practical understanding of international relations and logistics, preparing him for high ministerial office."], "effects": ["Champagny's administration as Minister of the Interior facilitated the implementation of the Napoleonic Code and the modernization of French infrastructure.", "His diplomatic efforts as Foreign Minister helped shape alliances and manage relations between the First French Empire and other major European powers, particularly Austria.", "Champagny's career exemplified the adaptability of French elites navigating successive regime changes from the Ancien Régime to the Bourbon Restoration."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "napoleon-bonaparte", "sourceName": "Napoleon Bonaparte", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompre-de-champagny", "targetName": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "context": "Napoleon Bonaparte's rise to power and his need for loyal administrators directly influenced Champagny's appointment to key ministerial roles from 1804 to 1811."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompre-de-champagny", "sourceName": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "continental-system", "targetName": "Continental System", "context": "As Minister of Foreign Affairs (1807–1811), Champagny was instrumental in implementing Napoleon's Continental System, aiming to economically isolate Great Britain."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompre-de-champagny", "sourceName": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "paris-france", "targetName": "Paris, France", "context": "Much of Champagny's significant political and diplomatic career as a minister under Napoleon occurred in Paris, the capital of the First French Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompre-de-champagny", "sourceName": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "charles-maurice-de-talleyrand-perigord", "targetName": "Charles Maurice de Talleyrand-Périgord", "context": "Champagny collaborated with Talleyrand on various diplomatic matters, notably during the negotiations that led to the Treaty of Tilsit in 1807, before succeeding him as Foreign Minister."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompre-de-champagny", "sourceName": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "napoleonic-wars", "targetName": "Napoleonic Wars", "context": "Champagny's diplomatic and administrative work as a minister was directly tied to and aimed at supporting France's efforts during the broader period of the Napoleonic Wars."}], "places": [{"name": "Roanne, France", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Ministerial Center"}, {"name": "Vienna, Austria", "role": "Diplomatic Engagement"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jean-baptiste-de-nompère-de-champagny"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1361368", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste_Nomp%C3%A8re_de_Champagny,_duc_de_Cadore"], "tags": ["France", "Political Leaders", "Europe", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny is remembered as a landmark person of the Early Modern era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped political institutions and governance practices; Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution. Their legacy is studied through the lens of political thought.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Champagny's ministerial roles under Napoleon were crucial for the effective implementation of the First French Empire's domestic reforms and foreign policy, shaping France's administrative structure and its diplomatic standing in Europe. His work contributed to the centralization of power and the expansion of French influence across the continent.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:28:32Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jean-Baptiste Nompère de Champagny, 1st duc de Cadore was a French admiral and politician.", "newValue": "Jean-Baptiste de Nompère de Champagny, 1st duc de Cadore, was a pivotal French statesman and naval officer whose career spanned the tumultuous years of the French Revolution, the First Empire, and the Bourbon Restoration. Born in Roanne on August 4, 1756, and dying on July 3, 1834, Champagny rose through the ranks of the Ancien Régime navy to become a trusted, albeit sometimes compliant, minister under Napoleon Bonaparte. His significance lies in his administrative acumen and diplomatic service, particularly as Minister of the Interior (1804–1807) and Minister of Foreign Affairs (1807–1811), where he implemented crucial Napoleonic reforms and navigated complex European alliances at the zenith of French power.

During his tenure as Minister of the Interior, Champagny oversaw the consolidation of the Napoleonic Code, the establishment of the Bank of France, and significant infrastructure projects, including canals and roads, that modernized France's internal administration and economy. His subsequent role as Minister of Foreign Affairs placed him at the heart of imperial diplomacy, where he negotiated treaties, managed relations with key European powers like Austria and Russia, and became an executor of Napoleon's Continental System. Notably, he played a role in the Franco-Austrian alliance sealed by Napoleon's marriage to Marie Louise in 1810, a strategic move intended to stabilize the empire.

Champagny's career reflects the broader transformations of French governance from monarchy to empire and back, showcasing the adaptability required of elites to survive and thrive across successive regimes. His administrative and diplomatic contributions helped solidify the institutional framework of the First Empire, many elements of which, like centralized administration and legal codes, endured beyond Napoleon's downfall. Though his influence waned after his dismissal in 1811, his legacy as a capable, if not always independently minded, servant of the state is undeniable."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:28:32Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1756-08-04, Roanne"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1756-08-04, Roanne"}], "newValue": ["The institutional reforms and meritocratic opportunities opened by the French Revolution allowed talented individuals like Champagny to rise rapidly in public service.", "Napoleon Bonaparte's consolidation of power after the Consulate created a demand for loyal and capable administrators to implement his ambitious domestic and foreign policies.", "Champagny's early naval career and experience abroad provided him with a practical understanding of international relations and logistics, preparing him for high ministerial office."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:28:32Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1834-07-03"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1834-07-03"}], "newValue": ["Champagny's administration as Minister of the In